{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject rather than describing it exactly when the test is installed into a fail-first adjudication loop before the subject's guard can observe the mutation: the 'shown-to-fail-first' installation reverses the temporal authority of guard and test, so mutations that are visible only to the test's own failure channel are registered as caught even when no guard fired. This is a masking-by-installation-order effect, not a property of the subject.","counterconditions":["If every guard had been run to completion before the first test-failure signal was committed, the 3-of-26 result would collapse toward a description of the guards, not the tests.","If at least one surviving mutation's guard had failed on a change visible only in the subject's declared data, the 'added behaviour' costume claim would fail.","If the same 3 survivors disappeared under a reordering where tests run after all guards, the installation-order explanation would be refuted."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12"},{"block":"028950f9751ab59a"}],"mechanism":"In the 3-of-26 result, the surviving mutations share a common cost: their guard checks declared data while the mutation adds behaviour. When a mutation is shown-to-fail first, the failing test is registered before the ordinarily-guarded module's guard is evaluated for that mutation, so the guard's failure to see the added behaviour is never counted. The 1/9 compile.py loss and the 4/4 to 6/7 per-module banding follow the same mechanism: modules whose guards fire on the failure-channel-adjacent surface are overcounted, while modules whose guards inspect a structurally disjoint surface (declared vs. added) are undercounted.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.72},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 3-of-26 result is a distributional artefact of mutation sampling: when the mutation set is authored by models that had already read the acceptance record, they produce mutations that cluster in the same blind spot the original guards missed, so a test constrains its subject only where the mutation prior overlaps the guard's firing surface.","counterconditions":["If a fresh, prior-blind mutation set reproduced 3-of-26, the sampling-artefact claim would lose its force.","If the 3 survivors were distributed uniformly across mutation categories, the clustering claim would fail.","If the authors' reading of the acceptance record could be shown not to have influenced their mutation choices, the mechanism would be inert."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc"}],"mechanism":"The caveat 'mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record' matters because the mutation prior is not uniform. A biased prior concentrates mutations where the guards are already weak, producing a survivor rate that measures the correlation between the prior and the guard surface rather than the guards' actual coverage. The compile.py 1/9 and every-module 4/4 to 6/7 banding are then the prior's shape, not the subject's.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.61},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The result is a tautology of the registry convention: the registry records what the tests say, not what the guards do, so under shown-to-fail-first installation the recorded distribution is a description of the test's own reach by construction, and any 'constraint' is only the registry's restatement of the test's failure channel.","counterconditions":["If at least one registry entry were written by a guard firing without a prior test failure, the by-construction claim would break.","If the registry distinguished test-caught from guard-caught, the tautology would be resolved into a description.","If a mutation could be recorded as lost even when the test passed and the guard fired, the registry would not merely restate the test."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"047055ae600ced87"}],"mechanism":"Because the installation ritual is shown-to-fail-first, the registry's entry for a mutation is written by the test's failure, not by the guard's verdict. The 3-of-26 count and the 1/9 compile.py loss are then restatements of which mutations the test channel can touch, and the 4/4 to 6/7 banding is the registry's serialisation of that channel. Nothing independent of the test is measured.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.58},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains rather than describes only when the subject's guard and the test's failure channel share an observable vocabulary; the 3-of-26 result marks the boundary of that shared vocabulary, and the banding 4/4 to 6/7 is the per-module size of the shared surface.","counterconditions":["If a guard and a test could constrain the same subject while observing disjoint vocabularies, the shared-vocabulary requirement would be false.","If a surviving mutation's observable signature were demonstrably inside the test's vocabulary, the boundary claim would fail.","If the banding 4/4 to 6/7 did not correlate with any measurable vocabulary overlap, the mechanism would lack support."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12"}],"mechanism":"Guards that check declared data share a vocabulary with tests that observe declared data; guards that check declared data but face added behaviour do not. The 3 survivors are exactly the mutations whose observable signature lies outside the shared vocabulary, so the test cannot constrain them. Compile.py's 1/9 and the ordinarily-guarded modules' 4/4 to 6/7 are the sizes of their respective shared surfaces.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.55},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The result is a confound of one repository, one author, and one week: the 3-of-26 distribution is too small and too